/P 363 0 R
/E (This workshop works best when it is interactive rather than lecture-based. Even if slides are used, facilitators should include questions, short reflections, examples, and moments where participants can share what they already know.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
865 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 363 0 R
/E (Before the workshop, it is useful to find out what topics are most interesting or relevant for the teenagers. This can be done through an anonymous form, a question box, or a short needs-assessment activity. This is especially important when working with participants from conservative backgrounds, where young people may have many questions but may not feel comfortable asking them openly.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
866 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 363 0 R
/E (Facilitators should create a respectful and emotionally safe atmosphere from the beginning. Participants should know that they do not have to share personal experiences and that they can participate silently if they prefer.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
867 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 363 0 R
/E (The use of anonymous questions is strongly recommended. It allows participants to ask about sensitive topics without fear of embarrassment, judgment, or exposure in front of peers.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
