876 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 365 0 R
/E (The project has demonstrated the strong potential of comprehensive sexuality education as a tool for empowerment, inclusion, and social change in youth work. Through collaborative work among participants from Italy, Sweden, Portugal, Spain, Germany, Lithuania, Poland, and Serbia, the Training Course created a valuable international space to exchange experiences, challenge taboos, and develop practical non-formal education tools that can be adapted to different cultural and local realities.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
877 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 365 0 R
/E (The workshops presented in this toolkit reflect the creativity, sensitivity, and commitment of the participating youth workers, educators, and facilitators. Each activity approaches sexuality education from a different perspective, including STI awareness, menstruation, consent, personal boundaries, safety, and inclusive dialogue. Together, they show that sexuality education is not only about sharing information, but also about creating safer spaces where young people can ask questions, reflect on their experiences, understand their rights, and learn to respect themselves and others.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
878 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 365 0 R
/E (A key lesson from the project is that sexuality education must be context-sensitive, inclusive, and participatory. Across Europe and beyond, young people grow up with different levels of access to reliable information, different cultural expectations, and different social norms around sexuality, gender, relationships, and the body. For this reason, youth workers need flexible tools that can be adapted to the needs, age, background, and comfort level of each group.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
879 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 365 0 R
/E (51)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
880 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 366 0 R
/T (This toolkit offers a small but meaningful collection of such tools. The activities were created and tested during the Training Course and are intended to support youth workers in opening conversations that are often avoided or treated as taboo. They encourage learning through dialogue, creativity, movement, reflection, and peer exchange, making them suitable for diverse youth work settings.)
/E (This toolkit offers a small but meaningful collection of such tools. The activities were created and tested during the Training Course and are intended to support youth workers in opening conversations that are often avoided or treated as taboo. They encourage learning through dialogue, creativity, movement, reflection, and peer exchange, making them suitable for diverse youth work settings.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
881 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 366 0 R
/T (Ultimately, the project highlights the role of youth workers as facilitators of trust, care, and critical thinking. By addressing sexuality education through respect, consent, inclusion, and dignity, youth work can contribute to healthier relationships, stronger self-awareness, reduced stigma, and more equal communities. The toolkit invites educators and youth workers to continue adapting, testing, and improving these methods in their own local contexts, helping young people across Europe and the world access the knowledge, confidence, and support they need.)
/E (Ultimately, the project highlights the role of youth workers as facilitators of trust, care, and critical thinking. By addressing sexuality education through respect, consent, inclusion, and dignity, youth work can contribute to healthier relationships, stronger self-awareness, reduced stigma, and more equal communities. The toolkit invites educators and youth workers to continue adapting, testing, and improving these methods in their own local contexts, helping young people across Europe and the world access the knowledge, confidence, and support they need.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
